ription without a hereditary or clinical dimension. So I'm drafting the principle formally — a loss-of-function head noun followed by a postmodifying prepositional phrase naming a gene/enzyme/protein, where the whole nominal denotes an inherited or clinical state, should be labeled as a single disease span.\n\nI'm also spelling out the negative constraints: skip this rule when the head is just a measurement noun, when the PP complement is a non-protein substance or anatomical site, when the nominal describes an assay/experimental manipulation rather than a patient condition, or when it's a bare symptom noun handled elsewhere. And I need to clarify that the gene/protein name itself, standing alone as the complement, stays unlabeled rather than being swept into the disease span.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Loss-of-State Nominal with Gene-Product Complement:**\n  - **IF** a loss-of-function state noun (a deficiency-type, absence-type, or deficient-activity nominal, with any adjectival premodifier) takes a postmodifying prepositional phrase whose object names a gene, enzyme, protein, or protein component, and the whole nominal denotes an inherited or clinical condition of the subject (often signaled by etiological or characterizing predicates such as \"caused by\" or \"characterized by\"),\n  - **THEN** label the entire nominal, from the head through the end of the prepositional complement, as SpecificDisease. The preposition does not block or shorten the span, and the label is the same whether the gene appears as a premodifier (\"[gene] deficiency\") or inside a prepositional complement.\n  - **DOES NOT APPLY** when: (a) the head denotes a measured quantity of the complement (e.g., activity, level, or concentration) with no loss-of-function state head; (b) the complement names a non-protein entity such as a nutrient, metabolite, toxin, or environmental agent; (c) the nominal describes an assay, experimental manipulation, or in-vitro condition rather than a